// A small FM sound engine for the demos: OPL2 (YM3812) voices, a
// sound-effect bank, a three-part music sequencer, and an SDL audio device
// to play them through. Bring your own instruments and note data — this
// file has no sounds of its own.
//
// The chip emulation is opl2.fc next door; this is the layer that turns it
// into game audio. Compile with `demos/shared/opl2.fc`,
// `demos/shared/sdl2.fc`, `stdlib/math.fc` (which opl2 needs), and
// `stdlib/io.fc` (one diagnostic, on bad song data).
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Threading — the part that matters
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Audio is generated by SDL's audio callback, on SDL's own thread, at the
// sound card's rate. It is NOT generated by the game loop and pushed with
// SDL_QueueAudio. That distinction is the whole reason this file exists:
// under the push model the game loop is the sample clock, so every frame
// that runs long or short bends the audio's timebase, and music comes out
// audibly wobbling in pitch and tempo. A callback asks for exactly the
// samples the device needs at exactly the moment it needs them, so a
// stuttering frame rate cannot reach the sound at all.
//
// The price of a callback is a second thread touching the chips. It is
// paid once, here, with a lock-free command ring: the game thread only
// ever *describes* what it wants ("play sound 3", "stop the music") by
// posting a command; the callback drains the ring and is the sole mutator
// of every chip, voice, and sequencer field. Nothing is shared but the
// ring, the game thread never blocks on the audio thread, and no lock is
// held anywhere. A full ring drops the request rather than stalling — a
// missed sound effect beats a hitched frame.
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Shutdown — deliberately absent
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// There is no close/destroy. See `shutdown_note` at the bottom of this
// file for why leaving the device open is the robust choice and not an
// oversight.
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// FM programming in one paragraph
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// A voice is two operators, a modulator and a carrier. In FM mode the
// modulator's output bends the carrier's phase, so the modulator's level
// (TL) is *brightness*, not volume, and the frequency ratio between the
// two (MULT) picks the timbre: 1:1 is a reedy sawtooth, 2:1 hollow, 3:1
// and up metallic and bell-like. Only the carrier's TL is volume, and TL
// counts *down* — 0 is loudest, 63 silent. Each operator runs a
// four-stage envelope (attack, decay, sustain, release) whose rates are
// 0..15, higher being faster; the EGT bit says whether a note holds at
// its sustain level (organ) or keeps decaying to nothing on its own
// (bell, pluck).

namespace shared::

// sdl2 and opl2 are siblings in `shared::`, so they need no import here.
import io from std::

// ============================================================
// Single-producer / single-consumer ring
// ============================================================

// A wait-free queue for exactly one producer thread and one consumer
// thread. The capacity is a const generic and must be a power of two, so
// the slot index is a mask rather than a modulo. Slots live inline, so a
// ring needs no allocation of its own: pass the address of a field, and
// the zero value (head == tail == 0) is already a valid empty ring.
module spsc =
    // The queue itself: 'cap slots inline, plus the two counters. `head` and
    // `tail` are free-running totals rather than wrapped indices, so their
    // difference is the occupancy and the empty and full states never look
    // alike. All-zero is a valid empty ring, which is why a ring embedded in
    // a zeroed struct needs no initialization step.
    struct ring =
        static_assert('cap > 0 && ('cap & ('cap - 1)) == 0,
            "spsc.ring capacity must be a power of two")
        buf: 'a['cap]
        head: usize      // consumer-owned: only the consumer advances it
        tail: usize      // producer-owned: only the producer advances it

    // Producer side. False means the ring is full and the caller should
    // drop the item; for audio that is a skipped sound, never a stall.
    let enqueue = (r: ring<'a, 'cap>*, v: 'a) ->
        let cap = (usize) 'cap
        let t = atomic_load_acquire(&r.tail)
        let h = atomic_load_acquire(&r.head)
        if t - h == cap then false
        else
            r.buf[t & (cap - 1usize)] = v
            atomic_store_release(&r.tail, t + 1usize)
            true

    // Consumer side. `none` means the ring is empty.
    let dequeue = (r: ring<'a, 'cap>*) ->
        let cap = (usize) 'cap
        let t = atomic_load_acquire(&r.tail)
        let h = atomic_load_acquire(&r.head)
        if t == h then none('a)
        else
            let v = r.buf[h & (cap - 1usize)]
            atomic_store_release(&r.head, h + 1usize)
            some(v)


// A small FM sound engine for the demos: OPL2 voices for sound effects, a
// three-part music sequencer, and an SDL audio device to play both through.
// It brings no sounds of its own — a game supplies a `bank` of effects and a
// `song`, both plain data.
//
// Audio is generated on SDL's audio callback thread, not pushed from the
// game loop, so a frame that runs long or short cannot bend the music's
// timebase. The price is a second thread touching the chips, and the whole
// threading contract exists to pay it: the game thread only ever *describes*
// what it wants by posting a command, and the callback drains that lock-free
// ring and is the sole mutator of every chip, voice, and sequencer field.
// Nothing else is shared, nothing blocks, and a full ring drops the request
// rather than stalling the frame.
//
// ```fc
// let a = opl_audio.init(rate, fx, tune, music_gain, sfx_gain)
// if opl_audio.start(a) then ...      // opens the device; false = no sound
// opl_audio.play(a, sound_id)
// ```
//
// Everything the game thread may call is in "The game thread's API" below —
// play, music_begin, music_end, music_hold, music_unhold, music_toggle_mute,
// music_muted. Nothing else on `audio` is safe to touch once the device is
// running. `render` is the offline path for tools and headless tests: it
// fills a buffer directly, through the same ring, with no device open.
//
// There is deliberately no close or destroy — see `shutdown_note` at the
// bottom of this file for why leaving the device open is the robust choice.
module opl_audio =

    // ============================================================
    // What a game supplies
    // ============================================================

    // An AdLib-style instrument: five register bytes for the modulator,
    // five for the carrier, and the feedback/connection byte the pair
    // shares. Each field is named for the register it lands in.
    struct instrument =
        m_char: i32     // 0x20  AM | VIB | EGT | KSR | MULT
        m_scale: i32    // 0x40  KSL<<6 | TL   — TL here is FM depth
        m_atk: i32      // 0x60  AR<<4 | DR
        m_sus: i32      // 0x80  SL<<4 | RR
        m_wave: i32     // 0xE0  waveform 0..3
        c_char: i32
        c_scale: i32    // 0x40  KSL<<6 | TL   — TL here is loudness
        c_atk: i32
        c_sus: i32
        c_wave: i32
        n_conn: i32     // 0xC0  FB<<1 | connection (0 = FM, 1 = additive)

    // One effect: an instrument, and a slice of the bank's flat step list.
    // Splitting the steps out of the struct is what lets every effect in a
    // game live in one readable array of (note, ticks) pairs.
    struct sound =
        instr: i32      // index into bank.instruments
        first: i32      // index of this sound's first step
        steps: i32      // how many steps it has

    // A game's whole effect set. `script` is (note, ticks) pairs, all
    // sounds' steps end to end; a note of -1 is a rest. A voice keys off
    // when its last step runs out, so the instrument's release rate — not
    // the script — decides how long the tail rings.
    struct bank =
        instruments: const instrument[]
        sounds: const sound[]
        script: const i32[]
        tick_rate: i32          // steps per second; 140 is the AdLib rate

    // A three-part tune: a melody over a bass line and an inner arpeggio.
    //
    // Only the melody carries note data. The other two parts are read off
    // `chords` — one chord index per half-bar — which is why a whole
    // arrangement costs little more than the tune itself.
    //
    // `grid` is the sequencer's resolution in steps per beat, and every
    // duration in the song is counted in those steps: 2 puts the grid on
    // eighth notes, 4 on sixteenths, 3 on beat-triplets. It buys
    // resolution, not range — a melody note is any whole number of steps,
    // so at grid 2 a quarter note is `2` and a dotted half is `6`.
    //
    // The loop length is not stated: it is `chords.len` half-bars, because
    // the chord table *is* the form. Nothing can disagree with itself.
    struct song =
        melody: const i32[]     // (note, length in grid steps) pairs; -1 = rest
        chords: const i32[]     // one chord index per half-bar
        roots: const i32[]      // bass note per chord index
        arps: const i32[]       // `arp_len` arpeggio notes per chord index
        grid: i32               // sequencer steps per beat
        arp_len: i32            // arpeggio notes per half-bar; 0 = no arpeggio
        bpm: i32
        lead: instrument
        bass: instrument
        arp: instrument

    // ============================================================
    // Tunables
    // ============================================================

    // Effect voices. Overlap is the point: a pop landing on top of a
    // still-ringing pop is what a chain reaction sounds like.
    let voices = 6

    // Device buffer, in frames. 1024 at 44.1 kHz is 23 ms of callback
    // latency. Smaller buffers shave that, but ask the host audio server
    // for service on a tighter deadline than some of them reliably meet.
    let device_frames = 1024

    // Everything meets in one i32 sum and leaves through a soft knee:
    // below `clip_knee` the sum passes untouched, above it the curve
    // bends toward full scale without ever reaching it. Gentle saturation
    // is what an analog mixer did with FM on a sound card, and it beats
    // the flat-topping a hard clamp gives when a fanfare and a fistful of
    // effects arrive together.
    private let clip_knee  = 24000
    private let clip_range = 8767      // 32767 - knee

    private let soft_clip = (s: i32) ->
        let mag = if s < 0 then 0 - s else s
        if mag <= clip_knee then s
        else
            // i64 for the product only: `over * range` overflows i32 past
            // |s| ~ 245k, while the quotient is bounded by range.
            let over = mag - clip_knee
            let out = clip_knee + (i32) ((i64) over * (i64) clip_range
                                          / (i64) (clip_range + over))
            if s < 0 then 0 - out else out

    // ============================================================
    // Pitch
    // ============================================================

    // A note is `block * 12 + semitone`, so note 48 is middle C and each
    // block is an octave. The F-numbers are one equal-tempered octave at
    // A = 440, rounded for the YM3812's
    //     f = fnum * 2^(block-1) * 49716 / 2^19.
    private let fnum_of = i32[12] {
        345, 365, 387, 410, 434, 460, 488, 517, 547, 580, 614, 651 }

    // Strike a note on a channel. The key-off first is not optional: the
    // chip only restarts an envelope on a *rising* key bit, so keying on a
    // channel that is already on would slide the pitch of the note already
    // sounding instead of articulating a new one.
    private let key_on = (c: opl2.chip*, ch: i32, note: i32) ->
        let n = if note < 0 then 0 else if note > 95 then 95 else note
        opl2.note_off(c, ch)
        opl2.note_on(c, ch, n / 12, fnum_of[n % 12])

    private let load = (c: opl2.chip*, ch: i32, ins: instrument) ->
        opl2.load_instrument(c, ch,
            ins.m_char, ins.c_char, ins.m_scale, ins.c_scale,
            ins.m_atk,  ins.c_atk,  ins.m_sus,   ins.c_sus,
            ins.m_wave, ins.c_wave, ins.n_conn)

    // ============================================================
    // Commands — the game thread's only way in
    // ============================================================

    // What a command asks the audio thread to do. The game thread never acts
    // on the engine directly — it posts one of these and returns.
    enum op =
        | play_sound
        | music_start       // from the top
        | music_stop        // and rewind
        | music_pause       // hold where it is
        | music_resume
        | music_set_mute    // arg: 1 = muted

    // One posted command: the op and its single i32 argument — a sound id
    // for play_sound, 1 or 0 for music_set_mute, unused otherwise. Small and
    // trivially copyable on purpose: it crosses the thread boundary by value
    // through the ring, so it can own no memory.
    struct cmd =
        kind: op
        arg: i32

    // ============================================================
    // State
    // ============================================================

    // Everything below `dev` is owned by the audio callback thread and
    // must not be touched from the game thread once the device is
    // unpaused. `ring` is the crossing point; `dev` and `muted` belong to
    // the game thread.
    struct audio =
        rate: i32
        music_chip: opl2.chip*
        sfx_chip: opl2.chip*
        fx: bank
        tune: song

        // Music sequencer
        music_want: bool        // the game wants music here
        music_mute: bool        // the player asked for silence
        music_live: bool        // what the two above add up to
        mus_accum: f64
        mus_period: f64         // samples per grid step
        pos: i32                // position in the loop, in grid steps
        mel_idx: i32            // index of the current melody event
        mel_left: i32           // grid steps left on the current melody note
        // Derived from the song at init, so the tick loop does no division.
        half_bar: i32           // grid steps per chord
        loop_len: i32           // grid steps in one time round

        // Effect voices
        sfx_accum: f64
        sfx_period: f64         // samples per effect tick
        v_active: bool[6]
        v_sound: i32[6]
        v_step: i32[6]
        v_left: i32[6]
        next_voice: i32

        // Per-source trim applied before the sum.
        music_gain: f64
        sfx_gain: f64

        // The crossing point.
        ring: spsc.ring<cmd, 64>

        // Game-thread side.
        dev: u32
        muted: bool             // mirror of music_mute, for the game's HUD

    // Music voices live on channels 0..2 of the music chip.
    private let ch_lead = 0
    private let ch_bass = 1
    private let ch_arp  = 2

    // Build the engine. The device is not open yet — call `start` once the
    // rest of the game is ready to be heard.
    let init = (sample_rate: i32, fx: bank, tune: song,
                music_gain: f64, sfx_gain: f64) ->
        let a = alloc(audio)!
        a.rate = sample_rate
        a.music_chip = opl2.init((f64) sample_rate)
        a.sfx_chip = opl2.init((f64) sample_rate)
        a.fx = fx
        a.tune = tune
        a.music_gain = music_gain
        a.sfx_gain = sfx_gain
        // One grid step is 60 / (bpm * grid) of a second.
        a.mus_period = (f64) sample_rate * 60.0 / (f64) (tune.bpm * tune.grid)
        a.sfx_period = (f64) sample_rate / (f64) fx.tick_rate
        // A half-bar is two beats — the span one chord covers.
        a.half_bar = tune.grid * 2
        a.loop_len = (i32) tune.chords.len * a.half_bar
        load(a.music_chip, ch_lead, tune.lead)
        load(a.music_chip, ch_bass, tune.bass)
        load(a.music_chip, ch_arp, tune.arp)

        // A melody longer than the chord progression is a data error with
        // no audible signature: the loop wrap restarts the melody, so the
        // overhanging notes are simply never reached and the tune is
        // silently truncated at the bar line. Catch it here, on the first
        // run, rather than leaving someone to wonder where the last phrase
        // went.
        //
        // A melody *shorter* than the loop is deliberately fine — it
        // repeats within the loop, which is how you write a two-bar figure
        // under an eight-bar progression without spelling it out four
        // times.
        //
        // This has to be a runtime check. The data is compile-time
        // constant, but summing it needs iteration, and FC's
        // constant-expression grammar admits no calls — so `static_assert`
        // cannot see it. Checked once at init, not per tick.
        let mut total = 0
        let mut ev = 0
        loop
            if (i64) (ev * 2 + 1) >= tune.melody.len then break
            total = total + tune.melody[ev * 2 + 1]
            ev = ev + 1
        if total > a.loop_len then
            io.write(alloca("opl_audio: melody runs %d{total} grid steps but the chord progression is only %d{a.loop_len} (%d{(i32) tune.chords.len} half-bars of %d{a.half_bar}); the tail would never be heard\n"), stderr)
        assert(total <= a.loop_len)
        a

    // ============================================================
    // Sequencers — audio thread only
    // ============================================================

    private let rewind = (a: audio*) ->
        a.pos = 0
        a.mel_idx = 0
        a.mel_left = 0
        // Primed, not zeroed: the first sample of the next callback is
        // then already an eighth-note boundary, so the tune starts on the
        // downbeat instead of after a silent eighth.
        a.mus_accum = a.mus_period

    // Fold `music_want` and `music_mute` into the one flag the mixer
    // reads, hushing the chip on the way down so a note can't hang. The
    // two inputs are separate on purpose: the game starts and stops the
    // tune around a run, the player mutes it, and neither should be able
    // to undo the other.
    private let sync_music = (a: audio*) ->
        let live = a.music_want && !a.music_mute
        if live != a.music_live then
            a.music_live = live
            if !live then
                opl2.silence_voices(a.music_chip)

    // One grid step. The melody runs off its own note list while the bass
    // and arpeggio read the chord under the current beat, which is why the
    // accompaniment needs no data beyond one chord index per half-bar.
    private let music_tick = (a: audio*) ->
        let t = a.tune
        let bar_pos = a.pos % a.half_bar        // step within this chord
        let bar = a.pos / a.half_bar
        let ci = if (i64) bar >= t.chords.len then (i32) t.chords.len - 1
                 else bar
        let chord = t.chords[ci]

        // Melody: advance the note list only when the held note expires. A
        // melody shorter than the loop wraps here and repeats.
        if a.mel_left <= 0 then
            if (i64) (a.mel_idx * 2) >= t.melody.len then
                a.mel_idx = 0
            let note = t.melody[a.mel_idx * 2]
            a.mel_left = t.melody[a.mel_idx * 2 + 1]
            a.mel_idx = a.mel_idx + 1
            if note >= 0 then
                key_on(a.music_chip, ch_lead, note)
            else
                opl2.note_off(a.music_chip, ch_lead)
        a.mel_left = a.mel_left - 1

        // Bass on the chord change.
        if bar_pos == 0 then
            key_on(a.music_chip, ch_bass, t.roots[chord])

        // Arpeggio: `arp_len` notes spread evenly across the half-bar, so
        // the same figure keeps its rhythm at any grid. Multiplying before
        // dividing is what keeps the index exact and in range for every
        // combination — `k` is strictly below `arp_len` because `bar_pos`
        // is strictly below `half_bar`. An `arp_len` that does not divide
        // `half_bar` still plays, just on fewer of its notes.
        if t.arp_len > 0 && bar_pos * t.arp_len % a.half_bar == 0 then
            let k = bar_pos * t.arp_len / a.half_bar
            key_on(a.music_chip, ch_arp, t.arps[chord * t.arp_len + k])

        a.pos = a.pos + 1
        if a.pos >= a.loop_len then
            a.pos = 0
            a.mel_idx = 0
            a.mel_left = 0

    // Fire an effect on the next voice by rotation.
    private let trigger = (a: audio*, id: i32) ->
        if id < 0 || (i64) id >= a.fx.sounds.len then return
        let sd = a.fx.sounds[id]
        if sd.steps <= 0 then return
        let v = a.next_voice
        a.next_voice = (a.next_voice + 1) % voices
        // Release whatever this voice was still ringing out *before*
        // reprogramming it: changing an operator's level or waveform under
        // a sounding note is an audible click.
        opl2.note_off(a.sfx_chip, v)
        load(a.sfx_chip, v, a.fx.instruments[sd.instr])
        a.v_sound[v] = id
        a.v_step[v] = 0
        a.v_active[v] = true
        a.v_left[v] = a.fx.script[sd.first * 2 + 1]
        key_on(a.sfx_chip, v, a.fx.script[sd.first * 2])

    private let sfx_tick = (a: audio*) ->
        for v in 0..voices do
            if a.v_active[v] then
                a.v_left[v] = a.v_left[v] - 1
                if a.v_left[v] <= 0 then
                    let sd = a.fx.sounds[a.v_sound[v]]
                    let step = a.v_step[v] + 1
                    a.v_step[v] = step
                    if step >= sd.steps then
                        // Script done: release the key and let the
                        // instrument's own tail finish the sound.
                        a.v_active[v] = false
                        opl2.note_off(a.sfx_chip, v)
                    else
                        let k = (sd.first + step) * 2
                        let note = a.fx.script[k]
                        a.v_left[v] = a.fx.script[k + 1]
                        if note >= 0 then
                            key_on(a.sfx_chip, v, note)
                        else
                            opl2.note_off(a.sfx_chip, v)

    // ============================================================
    // Mixing — audio thread only
    // ============================================================

    private let apply = (a: audio*, c: cmd) ->
        match c.kind with
        | play_sound -> trigger(a, c.arg)
        | music_start ->
            rewind(a)
            a.music_want = true
            sync_music(a)
        | music_stop ->
            a.music_want = false
            sync_music(a)
            rewind(a)
        | music_pause ->
            a.music_want = false
            sync_music(a)
        | music_resume ->
            a.music_want = true
            sync_music(a)
        | music_set_mute ->
            a.music_mute = c.arg != 0
            sync_music(a)

    // Apply every pending game-thread request. The single place any of the
    // state above is mutated, and it runs on one thread.
    private let drain = (a: audio*) ->
        loop
            match spsc.dequeue(&a.ring) with
            | some(c) -> apply(a, c)
            | none -> break

    private let fill = (a: audio*, out: i16[], frames: i32) ->
        for i in 0..frames do
            a.sfx_accum = a.sfx_accum + 1.0
            if a.sfx_accum >= a.sfx_period then
                a.sfx_accum = a.sfx_accum - a.sfx_period
                sfx_tick(a)
            let fx_out = opl2.sample(a.sfx_chip)
            let mut mus_out = 0
            if a.music_live then
                a.mus_accum = a.mus_accum + 1.0
                if a.mus_accum >= a.mus_period then
                    a.mus_accum = a.mus_accum - a.mus_period
                    music_tick(a)
                mus_out = opl2.sample(a.music_chip)
            let sum = (i32) ((f64) mus_out * a.music_gain)
                    + (i32) ((f64) fx_out * a.sfx_gain)
            out[i] = (i16) soft_clip(sum)

    // SDL's audio callback, running on SDL's own audio thread. It is the
    // sole mutator of the chips, voices and sequencers: the game thread
    // never touches them, it only posts onto `ring`, which this drains
    // here. That is what makes the whole thing lock-free — the game loop
    // never blocks waiting on audio, and audio never waits on a frame.
    //
    // Non-capturing (it references only module-level bindings), so
    // `&opl_audio.callback` is a plain C function pointer. `stream` is
    // typed u8* rather than any* so the emitted C matches
    // SDL_AudioCallback's `void (*)(void*, Uint8*, int)` exactly.
    let callback = (userdata: any*, stream: u8*, len: i32) ->
        let a = (audio*) userdata
        drain(a)
        let frames = len / 2          // mono i16: 2 bytes per frame
        let out = i16[] { ptr = (i16*) stream, len = (i64) frames }
        fill(a, out, frames)

    // ============================================================
    // Device
    // ============================================================

    // Open the device and start the callback. Returns false if no device
    // could be opened, in which case everything below is a silent no-op
    // and the game carries on without sound.
    //
    // Called after `init` has finished building the state, because the
    // callback starts reading that state the moment the device unpauses.
    let start = (a: audio*) ->
        let spec = sdl2.audio_spec {
            freq = a.rate, format = sdl2.audio_s16sys,
            channels = 1u8, silence = 0u8,
            samples = (u16) device_frames, padding = 0u16, size = 0u32,
            callback = &callback, userdata = (any*) a
        }
        let mut got = default(sdl2.audio_spec)
        a.dev = sdl2.open_audio(default(any*), 0, &spec, &got, 0)
        if a.dev == 0u32 then
            false
        else
            sdl2.pause_audio(a.dev, 0)
            true

    // ============================================================
    // The game thread's API — posts commands, touches nothing else
    // ============================================================

    // A full ring drops the request. That is the correct trade for audio:
    // the alternative is making a frame wait on the audio thread, and a
    // sound nobody hears costs less than a frame nobody sees.
    private let post = (a: audio*, kind: op, arg: i32) ->
        ignore spsc.enqueue(&a.ring, cmd { kind = kind, arg = arg })

    // Play a sound effect: `id` indexes the bank's `sounds`. Voices are
    // handed out round-robin, so a new effect eventually steals the oldest
    // one still ringing; an id outside the bank is ignored rather than an
    // error. Returns nothing and never blocks — the sound starts when the
    // audio thread next drains the ring.
    let play = (a: audio*, id: i32) -> post(a, op.play_sound, id)

    // Start the tune from the top.
    let music_begin = (a: audio*) -> post(a, op.music_start, 0)

    // Stop it, and rewind so the next run opens on the first bar.
    let music_end = (a: audio*) -> post(a, op.music_stop, 0)

    // Hold it where it is — a pause, not a stop: the position is kept.
    let music_hold = (a: audio*) -> post(a, op.music_pause, 0)

    // Pick it back up from where music_hold left it.
    let music_unhold = (a: audio*) -> post(a, op.music_resume, 0)

    // The player's own switch, independent of the game's start/stop.
    let music_toggle_mute = (a: audio*) ->
        a.muted = !a.muted
        post(a, op.music_set_mute, if a.muted then 1 else 0)

    // Whether the player's mute switch is on — the game thread's own mirror
    // of it, readable straight from a HUD without touching audio-thread
    // state. Independent of whether the game currently wants music playing.
    let music_muted = (a: audio*) -> a.muted

    // ============================================================
    // Offline rendering
    // ============================================================

    // Produce `frames` samples into `out` with no device involved, running
    // the command ring and both sequencers exactly as the callback does.
    // For tools that write the audio to a file and for headless tests: it
    // exercises the real producer -> ring -> consumer path rather than a
    // bypass. Do not call it while a device is open — the callback is
    // already the consumer, and this would be a second one.
    let render = (a: audio*, out: i16[], frames: i32) ->
        drain(a)
        fill(a, out, frames)

    // ============================================================
    // shutdown_note — why there is no close/destroy here
    // ============================================================
    //
    // SDL_CloseAudioDevice *joins* the audio callback thread, and so does
    // SDL_Quit, which closes the device on the way out. If that thread is
    // wedged — a host audio server losing a sink mid-session is the usual
    // way — the join never returns, and an exiting game hangs forever on
    // its way out the door. SDL offers no close-with-timeout, so the only
    // robust shutdown is not to join at all: let the process exit and the
    // kernel reap the thread, the device, and the memory. Closing the
    // audio connection by process death also releases the device cleanly
    // for the next launch.
    //
    // The consequence for callers: **do not call SDL_CloseAudioDevice or
    // SDL_Quit**, and do not free anything reachable from `audio` while
    // the device is open — a live callback could read a chip mid-free.
    // Tearing down the window and renderer is fine and still worth doing;
    // that is video, which an audio wedge cannot block.
